WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.240
- Is it possible to sound gay?

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:04.560
We know many of the factors

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:05.610
that shape our identities

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:08.070
from race to geography to disability

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.050
can impact the way we use language.

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:13.170
So what about gender and sexuality?

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.990
Lavender language refers
to the speech practices

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:19.440
of LGBTQ+ people and the
linguists who study it

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.660
have chronicled a rich global history

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.540
that has thrived in secrecy for decades.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:25.890
I'm Dr. Erica Brozovsky

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:27.486
and this is "Other Words."

00:00:27.486 --> 00:00:30.653
(bright quirky music)

00:00:34.803 --> 00:00:37.136
- [Announcer] "Other Words."

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:39.690
- In the 1990s,

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.640
anthropologist William Leap

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.050
wanted to study the way people

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:45.900
of different LGBTQ+ identities speak

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.310
and chose lavender,

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:50.940
a slang synonym for gay or
queer, as an umbrella term.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:54.030
Leap wasn't the first to
study lavender language.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:54.990
The term he used

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:56.670
came from a dictionary of gay slang

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.100
written in the 1940s.

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:00.960
But his work defined a field of study

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.430
that covers everything

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:04.440
from how people use pronouns

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.720
to the wordplay of gossip and insults

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.030
to queer vernaculars or argots,

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:11.700
the complex and sometimes
secret vocabularies

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.487
of LGBTQ+ communities.

00:01:13.487 --> 00:01:14.940
One of the most famous

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.730
and well-documented gay argots is Polari,

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.500
which was used by gay men in England

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.840
until the late 1960s.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.060
It borrowed from all
kinds of speaking groups

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:25.650
on the fringes of society

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:27.720
like thieves, sailors,

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.120
theater performers, and Romani people.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:32.820
Polari speakers would
rhyme or reverse words

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:36.120
or borrow from other
languages to create new slang.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.040
Some Polari words that we still use

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.950
are naff, meaning tacky or unfashionable,

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:44.010
or zhuzh, the verb for
sprucing up your hair or outfit

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.860
that's fun to say, but
impossible to spell.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.090
In the late '60s,

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:49.260
Polari started to show up

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.840
in popular music and television shows,

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.820
sort of like how contemporary queer

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.320
and trans ballroom slang

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:58.440
has gotten popularized through
shows like "Drag Race."

00:01:58.440 --> 00:01:59.700
At the same time,

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.520
Britain repealed part of
its anti-homosexuality laws,

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.160
making the secret code
less necessary for safety.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.250
Polari started to fall out of regular use

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:08.550
in the gay community,

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:10.170
suffering a significant blow

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:12.180
when many of the
remaining Polaris speakers

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.820
died in the AIDS crisis of the 1980s.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:17.040
Still, lavender linguists continued

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:18.840
to study and document Polari

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.400
to preserve this important part

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.230
of LGBTQ+ history.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:23.490
But for much of its history,

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:25.950
Polari was somewhat of a secret code.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:28.650
When homosexuality was
criminalized in the UK,

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.260
slipping Polari phrases
into everyday speech

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:34.590
could help you identify who
else around you was also gay

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:36.840
without being obvious to straight people.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.910
Polari also had slang names for police

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:41.400
like Lily Law or Jennifer Justice,

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:42.840
so that gay men could warn each other

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.270
that police were around
without them noticing.

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:47.310
Some features of Polari also live on

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:50.340
in other lavender
languages around the globe.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:52.770
Loxoro, an argot based on Spanish

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:55.470
spoken by trans people
and sex workers in Peru,

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:57.330
has a similar coded nature,

00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:59.580
reversing words and adding extra syllables

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:01.230
like dosoro and cuti.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:03.300
Hola becomes hosorolasara.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.730
And Mama becomes Macuti.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.250
This feature called cryptolalisation

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:11.610
allows Loxoro speakers
to discuss taboo topics

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:14.430
around listeners they
may not know or trust.

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:16.170
Gayle, an argot that arose

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:17.430
in apartheid South Africa

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.020
among gay men of color,

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:20.490
also borrows from Polari

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.160
and mixes it with English and Afrikaans.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.440
Gayle's main feature is using alliteration

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:29.070
to substitute women's names
for other common words.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:32.580
Just like Polari referred to
police as Jennifer Justice,

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:35.340
Gayle speakers might talk
about avoiding Betty Bangles

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.320
or getting Dora at a patsy

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:39.150
instead of drinking at a party.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:40.650
Gayle, like Polari,

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:43.590
employs a common feature
of lavender languages.

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.020
She-ing, or using feminine language

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:47.520
for non feminine subjects.

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.380
Just like how gay men in the US

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:52.200
might refer to friends as
girl, sister, or queen.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.210
Lavender languages have a long tradition

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:55.770
of playfulness with gender

00:03:55.770 --> 00:03:58.530
to challenge our ideas
about language and power.

00:03:58.530 --> 00:03:59.850
Using feminine language

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:01.770
reverses the cultural hierarchies

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:02.970
that put masculine people

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:04.560
at the top of the social order

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:07.140
and feminine, queer, and
trans people at the bottom.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.510
According to lavender linguist Paul Baker,

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:12.750
she-ing is, "Inverting
mainstream society's values

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:14.400
so that everybody's potentially gay

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.320
and everybody's potentially feminine."

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.420
That's also how speakers of Swardspeak,

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.180
also called simply gay
lingo in the Philippines,

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:23.220
describe their use of language.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.620
The mix of Tagalog, Spanish, English

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:28.410
and some Japanese that began in the 1970s

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:31.380
is known as a mode of social resistance

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:33.720
for the way that it
allows speakers to play

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:34.920
with gendered language rules

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:36.240
of the languages they speak.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.340
Like the other argots we discussed,

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:40.590
Swardspeak is used to immediately identify

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:42.210
other speakers as queer,

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:43.830
and it can also be pretty useful

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:46.470
for gossiping or talking
behind people's backs

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:48.810
in a way that not
everybody can understand.

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:52.200
But speakers say it's no longer
as secret as it used to be.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.940
In fact, in circles that contain

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:55.770
a lot of LGBTQ+ people,

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:58.770
like the fashion, beauty,
and entertainment industries,

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:00.150
Swardspeak has spread to women

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:01.650
and even some straight men.

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:04.800
Swardspeak is not just
a multilingual mashup.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.710
It's also filled with
celebrity references.

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:10.860
You might call an inexpensive
outfit Mariah Carey,

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:12.990
because Mariah sounds like mura,

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:14.700
the Tagalog word for cheap.

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:17.070
And Julanis Morissette means to rain

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:19.260
from the Tagalog word for rain, ulan,

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:21.900
and the iconic line
from the song "Ironic."

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:23.970
Using Swardspeak requires a lot

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:26.190
of complex cultural knowledge.

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:28.740
There are borrowed words
from half a dozen languages

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:30.420
and deep pop culture cuts

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:31.800
that can take speakers years

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.150
of engaging with the gay community

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:35.520
to be able to fully understand.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.280
This helps solidify the
in-group identities of speakers

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:40.380
through the act of
creating language together.

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:41.970
Some Swardspeak users say

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:44.700
that gay lingo is like verbal jazz

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:46.380
where speakers are constantly riffing

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:47.880
and innovating off of each other

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:49.980
to keep this lavender language evolving.

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:51.180
These are by no means

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:53.580
the only lavender languages that exist.

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:54.840
As the field grows,

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:56.850
researchers from all over the world

00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:58.950
gather each year to talk
about new developments

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:01.050
in LGBTQ+ language use

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:02.580
and there's so much more to learn.

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:04.680
Many of the examples we discussed today

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:08.040
are lavender languages
used by cisgender gay men

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:10.950
because they're some of
the most researched argots.

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:13.920
Still, trans and gender
nonconforming people

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.050
are also innovating language practices

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:18.930
like repopularizing the
use of the singular they,

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:20.670
or introducing themselves with pronouns

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:21.780
when meeting new people.

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:23.580
While there's so much more to study,

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:25.680
lavender linguistics reminds us

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:28.110
that celebrating our own
identities is a doorway

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:31.560
into a freer and more expansive
expression of ourselves

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:34.020
and that there is no more
direct means of expression

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:35.822
than language.

00:06:35.822 --> 00:06:37.736
(bright quirky music)

00:06:37.736 --> 00:06:40.653
(Erica stuttering)

00:06:42.286 --> 00:06:45.036
(Erica laughing)

00:06:47.999 --> 00:06:50.582
